{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"distinct-technocrat-silkworm","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# 88d9's inbound parent-clear is unreachable in a bidirectional pass: outbound re-pushes the parent and inbound discards its own clear\n\nA parent cleared on the Jira side is never cleared locally. The inbound clear that ticket\n88d9 built is COMPUTED and then DISCARDED within the same pass, so `.parent_id` survives\nindefinitely on a child whose remote parent is gone.\n\n## Proven RED-first, not inferred\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_parent_clear_bidirectional_37e7.py` wires the REAL\noutbound differ into the REAL inbound differ the way `run_differs` does — deliberately NOT a\nhand-built outbound entry, which is the flaw that hid this. Two cells, first run, no setup\nfighting:\n\n    test_real_outbound_differ_emits_a_parent_write_for_the_deparented_child   PASSED\n    test_inbound_clear_survives_a_real_bidirectional_pass                     FAILED\n\n      outbound fields for DC-1: {'parent': 'DC-2'}\n      inbound fields for DC-1:  {}\n      inbound items suppressed by outbound context: 1\n      RECON: outbound_update key=DC-1 changed=[parent] comments=0 labels=0 links=0\n\n`suppressed == 1` is the smoking gun: inbound computed exactly one item — the clear — and it\nwas thrown away.\n\n## Mechanism, both steps read from source\n\n1. **Outbound re-pushes the parent.** `outbound_field_diff._resolve_local_parent` resolves the\n   bound epic parent; `canonical_remote[\"remote_parent_id\"]` is now `None`; they differ, so the\n   local-wins branch emits `changed[\"parent\"] = <parent key>`. Crucially `parent` is NOT in\n   `_INBOUND_MIRRORED_FIELDS`, so no baseline arbitration ever defers to inbound on this field.\n2. **Inbound discards its own clear.** `inbound_differ` canonicalizes the outbound `parent` to\n   `parent_id`, then the contradiction filter drops every key already present in the outbound\n   entry — including the clear it just computed.\n\nThe clear's precondition (Jira has no parent, local does) is IDENTICAL to the outbound\nre-push's precondition. So for any parent rebar can resolve outbound — bound, and epic-typed\nper bug 8b25's guard — 88d9's clear can never fire in a bidirectional pass.\n\n## Why the existing 88d9 suite is green\n\nEvery 88d9 acceptance cell passes `outbound_mutations=None`. The single cell that DOES supply\noutbound context — `test_same_pass_outbound_parent_write_suppresses_the_clear` — hand-builds a\nfake outbound entry and asserts the suppression as DESIRED behaviour. It never asks whether the\nreal outbound differ produces that mutation in the clear scenario. It always does.\n\nThat is this epic's signature failure shape one level up: not a vacuous oracle, but a REAL\noracle asserting the wrong thing because its input was fabricated rather than produced.\n\n## Scope\n\nConfirmed for the EPIC-parent case, which is the normal one on Data Center. For a non-epic\nlocal parent `_resolve_local_parent` omits the parent entirely, outbound emits nothing, and the\nsuppression cannot fire — that is the separate situation [rebar:39c1-2a32-b564-4b4b] covers.\n\n## Blast radius beyond DC\n\nThe suppression lives in the SHARED differ layer, not in a vendor adapter, so Jira Cloud is\nimplicated on the same code path wherever a parent is cleared remotely while the local parent\nstill resolves. Not yet observed there — recorded as a question, not a claim.\n\n## Proposed fix (from the trace, not yet implemented)\n\nGive the outbound parent branch the directionality guard the mirrored fields already have,\nreusing 88d9's own evidence channel: before emitting a parent SET, suppress when `local_parent`\nis truthy, `remote_parent` is falsy, AND `binding_store.get_peer_parent(local_id) ==\nlocal_parent` — i.e. the peer de-parented since our last observation while local stood still,\nwhich is exactly the case `diff_inbound_parent` exists to handle. `get_peer_parent` must stay\ngetattr-guarded, and its absence must fall through to today's local-wins push.\n\nGuard 4 (`test_same_pass_outbound_parent_write_suppresses_the_clear`) then needs restating as\n\"an outbound push suppresses the clear ONLY when local re-set the parent\", rather than\nunconditionally.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `test_inbound_clear_survives_a_real_bidirectional_pass` is GREEN\n- [ ] a local parent that was genuinely re-set still wins outbound — the fix must not invert\n      into \"remote always wins\", which would silently drop real local intent\n- [ ] `test_dc_mutations.py::test_inbound_clear_round_trips` is GREEN on a live harness run,\n      with the run id recorded\n- [ ] the reworded guard 4 asserts suppression only for the local-re-set case","id":"339a-57ac-e5f3-4718","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"88d9's inbound parent-clear is unreachable in a bidirectional pass: outbound re-pushes the parent and inbound discards its own clear"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785810896142031001,"uuid":"72db03ac-e19c-4c69-a2d2-b38db484e955"}